            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-f43f54b5a040bb5f7af3\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"Blood type (blood group) is an inherited classification based on antigens—markers—on red blood cells, and it matters because a recipient’s immune system can react to incompatible donor cells. The best-known system, ABO, sorts blood into A, B, AB, or O according to whether red cells carry A antigens, B antigens, both, or neither. A second major system is RhD: the D antigen’s presence is called positive and its absence negative, creating familiar labels such as A+ and O−. Together ABO and RhD make eight common combinations. Many other red-cell antigens also exist, so “universal donor” is shorthand for O-negative red cells, not a substitute for medical testing and matching.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nhs.uk/tests-and-treatments/blood-groups/\",\"https://stanfordbloodcenter.org/donate-blood/blood-donation-facts/blood-types/\",\"https://prod-www.redcrossblood.org/donate-blood/blood-types.html\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"Blood groups are inherited from parents.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nhs.uk/tests-and-treatments/blood-groups/\",\"https://prod-www.redcrossblood.org/donate-blood/blood-types.html\"]},{\"text\":\"In the ABO system, A and B describe which of those antigens are present on red cells: A has A, B has B, AB has both, and O has neither.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nhs.uk/tests-and-treatments/blood-groups/\",\"https://stanfordbloodcenter.org/donate-blood/blood-donation-facts/blood-types/\"]},{\"text\":\"RhD-positive red cells have the D antigen; RhD-negative red cells lack it.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nhs.uk/tests-and-treatments/blood-groups/\"]},{\"text\":\"Combining ABO and RhD gives eight common labels, such as A+, O−, and AB+.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nhs.uk/tests-and-treatments/blood-groups/\",\"https://stanfordbloodcenter.org/donate-blood/blood-donation-facts/blood-types/\"]},{\"text\":\"O-negative is called the universal red-cell donor type, but other blood-group antigens mean transfusions still require medical testing and matching.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nhs.uk/tests-and-treatments/blood-groups/\",\"https://prod-www.redcrossblood.org/donate-blood/blood-types.html\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Blood groups\",\"url\":\"https://www.nhs.uk/tests-and-treatments/blood-groups/\",\"publisher\":\"NHS\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:51:48.841595+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Inheritance; ABO antigen patterns; RhD-positive/negative distinction; eight common groups; blood testing and transfusion matching.\"},{\"title\":\"Blood Types\",\"url\":\"https://stanfordbloodcenter.org/donate-blood/blood-donation-facts/blood-types/\",\"publisher\":\"Stanford Blood Center\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:51:48.841595+00:00\",\"supports\":\"ABO classification by A and B red-cell antigens; Rh status; eight common combinations.\"},{\"title\":\"Blood Types Explained - A, B, AB and O\",\"url\":\"https://prod-www.redcrossblood.org/donate-blood/blood-types.html\",\"publisher\":\"American National Red Cross\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:51:48.841595+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Inherited blood type; antigen-based classification; O-negative as universal red-cell donor; other antigens and the need for careful typing and cross-matching.\"}],\"research_queries\":[\"site:medlineplus.gov blood typing ABO Rh antigens blood transfusion\",\"site:stanfordbloodcenter.org blood types ABO Rh antigens transfusion universal donor\",\"site:nhs.uk blood groups ABO Rh blood transfusion\",\"site:redcrossblood.org blood types ABO Rh antigen universal donor O negative\",\"site:blood.ca blood types antigens ABO Rh transfusion matching\",\"site:stanfordbloodcenter.org/donate-blood/blood-donation-facts/blood-types ABO Rh\"],\"notes\":\"Overview is one original paragraph of 97 words. Directly opened and read all three cited pages. The universal-donor point is explicitly limited to red cells; no claim is made that any blood type is universally compatible for every blood component or situation.\",\"unresolved_concerns\":[],\"self_check_complete\":true}"
