    "overview": {
      "text": "Blood type (blood group) is an inherited classification based on antigens—markers—on red blood cells, and it matters because a recipient’s immune system can react to incompatible donor cells. The best-known system, ABO, sorts blood into A, B, AB, or O according to whether red cells carry A antigens, B antigens, both, or neither. A second major system is RhD: the D antigen’s presence is called positive and its absence negative, creating familiar labels such as A+ and O−. Together ABO and RhD make eight common combinations. Many other red-cell antigens also exist, so “universal donor” is shorthand for O-negative red cells, not a substitute for medical testing and matching.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nhs.uk/tests-and-treatments/blood-groups/",
        "https://stanfordbloodcenter.org/donate-blood/blood-donation-facts/blood-types/",
        "https://prod-www.redcrossblood.org/donate-blood/blood-types.html"
      ]
    },
    "key_facts": [
